     1	# Chapter 1 — Unmeasured
     2	
     3	*Second day past the Line.*
     4	
     5	*I'm dating this by the Line because there is nothing else out here to date it by. No bell, no term, no board with a week written on it. Karis says a calendar is an institution too, and she is right, so: two days.*
     6	
     7	*Walked eleven miles. The road is real. It has ruts, and the ruts are a cart's width. The carters call these the void roads. I have been reading that phrase off maps since I was fourteen — dashes, ending in nothing. It is a road. It has ruts.*
     8	
     9	*Inventory, because I do one at the start of anything. I am eighteen. Nine confirmed: Wind, Pressure, Iron, Compression, Ember, Storm, Anchor, Blade, and Shadow, which costs from waking whether I use it or not and drifted twice today on a road with nobody on it. Deployment since the Line: none. One anomaly. Session nine. Still open. Still real. Patience.*
    10	
    11	*No tier. No rank. No enrollment. And — I checked this morning from the top of the shelf, both directions, every morning for four years now — no file I can see.*
    12	
    13	*Fourth day. A ford.*
    14	
    15	*The last of the westbound couriers turned back here: a woman on a mule with a satchel of post, who took the two letters I have carried since the first camp and said this was as far as post went. Past the ford it is carters or nothing. She looked at the five of us and did not ask what we were. I waited for the question the whole time she was tightening her girth. It did not come. I am writing down that it did not come.*
    16	
    17	*Sixth day.*
    18	
    19	*Fell in behind three carts going east and watched them. The carters walk quiet. Not silent — quiet: no talk on the road, no singing, a word to the mule at need. Steel where it can be reached: a crossbow across the sacks, a bill-hook by the driver's knee. No fire until the road is behind them for the night, and then a small one. And when a boy on the second cart — sixteen, maybe — put his hand out toward a hillside as though he meant to try something, the driver knocked the hand down without looking round and said, "Not on the road."*
    20	
    21	*He did not say why. I asked at the fire. He said, "You're paper, aren't you," which is what they call anyone from inside the Line, and I said we were, and he said, "Then you know how to keep quiet. Keep it." I have had longer conversations with clerks and learned less.*
    22	
    23	*So, the etiquette, as observed and not as explained: walk quiet. Carry steel. Don't light anything you don't need. Don't declare. Nobody has given me the reason for any of it and I am not going to make one up. Claim: there is a reason. Evidence: three carters and a boy's hand knocked down. Ruling: hold.*
    24	
    25	*Ninth day. Lowmarch.*
    26	
    27	*A river, wide and brown, with a ford marked by poles and a rope ferry for the deep part. Two inns, priced for people with more coin than we have. A smith, working. No wall. A meadow above the ford full of carts and tents, which is where anyone who is not a carter sleeps, and where we are. The ferryman told Brom that half the faces in the meadow will be gone by the month's end and the other half will be new. Nobody here has been here long enough to be anybody.*
    28	
    29	*The board is under a roof on four posts by the ferry landing: planks nailed to the posts at head height, slips of paper pinned to the planks. I read it like the Cinder House board at fourteen: all of it, twice, and then sorted.*
    30	
    31	*Fourteen postings. Six escorts. Three clearances — two fence-lines, one ford. Two recoveries — a cart in a wash, a load off a bank. Three hold contracts, priced high, with a word on each in the board-keeper's hand: HOLD, meaning a crew is on it. Every slip prices the job. Not one slip prices the crew. There is no line on any of them for what a crew has to be — no "Iron or above," no "certified," nothing — and I looked for it the way you look for a stair in the dark, with a foot, because every board I have ever read had it.*
    32	
    33	*Sorted by what each would cost the five of us: the escorts cost time. The fence-lines cost Brom's back. The wash costs rope and Lira. The ford clearance I can't price, because I don't know what is in the ford.*
    34	
    35	*Pike keeps the board. Sixties. One arm — the left sleeve pinned flat by someone who has done it every morning for years and stopped thinking about it. He sits like a Force practitioner who has stopped using it, weight all the way down. He said "Copper, Force, thirty years on the roads" in the same breath as his name, as a man says his trade, and did not ask ours. He asked what we could do. Lira said "Walk, carry, climb, fight," and he said "In that order?" and she said "Depends on the day," and he wrote something down.*
    36	
    37	*He opened a heading for us. Five names down the margin of a fresh page, in his hand, spelled as we say them. Five columns ruled beside the names: Job. Hirer. Witness. Outcome. Price. I read it upside down, because I read everything upside down, and then read it again to be sure I had not missed the column every ledger I have ever been entered in has had, the one that says what you are. I had not missed it. The page does not have it.*
    38	
    39	*Fourteenth day.*
    40	
    41	*First contract. Ferry escort: a day on the ferry, six crossings, with a broker's bales and a broker who wanted five people visible on his goods. Nothing happened. Two marks for standing where we could be seen, which is a job I have done before, in a gallery, at a higher price and for worse people. Pike's line, read upside down: escort, ferry, six crossings, no loss, two marks. The broker made his mark under "witness." The line does not say who we are. It says what we did.*
    42	
    43	*Twenty-second day.*
    44	
    45	*Second contract. Fence-line clearance at a steading a half-day north — a mile of posts choked in thorn, too few people to cut it before the stock came down. Three days, bill-hooks and gloves. Brom cut for six hours without stopping. Karis lit nothing; she was asked not to, and did not ask why, and wrote the not-asking in her notebook, underlined. Four marks and a sack of flour, and the steading's woman said "Cheap," to nobody, in the tone of a woman who intends to hire us again.*
    46	
    47	*Thirty-third day.*
    48	
    49	*Third contract. A carter's cart gone into a wash on the north road, nose down, one wheel in the water. Rope. Lira on the far bank because she is the one who can get to a far bank; Brom on the near one because he is the one who can hold it. Two hours, and the cart came out with three wheels. Three marks. The carter looked at Brom for a while afterward and said "You're not paper," and Brom said "I am," and the carter shrugged and paid.*
    50	
    51	*Three contracts. Nine marks and some flour. Nobody has been hurt and nobody has been asked anything. The unit is finding its feet in a country that has never seen an academy, and the feet are slow, and the feet are ours.*
    52	
    53	*Note, because a thing I keep noticing goes in the log: nobody is watching. I have counted every morning — the far bank, the meadow's edge, the road both ways — and the count is zero, and I keep counting, because the counting is the last thing the watchers left me and I have not decided yet whether to put it down.*
    54	
