    72	
    73	"You walk ahead of me," the carter said. "Not far. Quiet — no talk on the stretch. Nothing lit. Steel where you can reach it. And you don't declare. Not on that stretch. Not for anything."
    74	
    75	"What if something—" Lira began.
    76	
    77	"You don't declare."
    78	
    79	From the far side of the fire, where he had been sitting long enough that the carter had not counted him, Seln said, "Understood."
    80	
    81	The carter looked at Seln for the first time, and nodded once. "Sunrise," he said. "The meadow gate. Cart's got room on top of the sacks if you've a load."
    82	
    83	They had a load. Karis's three boxes went up onto the grain at first light, roped by Brom and checked by Karis at every halt for two days, and Seln's locked case rode where it always rode, on Seln.
    84	
    85	---
    86	
    87	The stretch was the last two miles of the road, and the carter had timed the day so they would reach it with light to spare, and the day had not cooperated. A wheel had found a stone on the morning's bench and taken an hour and a wedge to answer for it. So they came down off the shelf into the valley with the sun already behind the hills at their backs and the light going the grey that light went when it had stopped being anyone's.
    88	
    89	Cael read the ground as he would have read a floor.
    90	
    91	The road ran east, straight, down a long shallow valley whose floor was scrub — grey-green, thorned, knee-high near the road and chest-high thirty paces off it, dense on the right-hand side and thinning to the left, where the ground fell away in gravel to a dry wash. The wash ran beside the road the whole length of the stretch, ten or twelve paces off. On the right there was nothing but scrub to the valley wall, a quarter mile of it, dark already. Two miles ahead, where the valley closed, there was a line that was not scrub, and one light on it.
    92	
    93	The order had settled itself on the first day without a word from anyone, because two years of walking into rooms together had made it a habit. Lira went first, twenty paces ahead, on the balls of her feet. Cael and Karis came next, five paces ahead of the mule, on the crown of the road. Brom walked at the mule's near shoulder with one hand on the cheek-strap, because the carter had asked for a big man at the mule's head and had not said why, and because the mule had decided about Brom on the first morning. The carter walked at the off side with the rein. Seln was on the left, off the road, along the wash's lip, in the place two years had taught Cael to look for him last.
    94	
    95	The valley was making noise. He noticed that only afterward, when it stopped — the small continuous racket of a scrubland at dusk, insects, something dry moving in the thorn, a bird somewhere on the right saying the same two notes at intervals. The mule's harness had its own noise, a leather creak and a buckle that had not been set, which Cael had stopped trying to stop hearing.
    96	
    97	They had gone perhaps half a mile into the stretch when Lira stopped and put her hand up.
    98	
    99	She did not turn. Her hand went up open, palm back, the way it went up on a floor when she wanted a drill halted and did not want to spend breath on it, and she stood where she had stopped with her weight forward and did not move anything else.
