   112	
   113	Lira's hand came down. She walked. The mule walked, because Brom did. Cael walked, and did the thing he had trained himself to do on floors where the other man had already moved and he had not yet seen how, which was to catalogue instead of react.
   114	
   115	He found it on the second look. Forty meters off the road on the right, in the chest-high scrub, a place where the grey was a different grey — lower than the thorn, longer than a dog, so exactly the valley's colour that he lost it the moment he looked straight at it and found it again only at the edge of his eye. It was moving. It was moving when they moved, at their pace, and when he watched it for fifty paces it neither gained a stride nor lost one.
   116	
