     3	They came back down into Lowmarch on the forty-ninth day, at midday, with the Thornwater road behind them and nothing on it.
     4	
     5	Seln had set the hour. He had said, the evening before, at Thornwater's fence, that they would cross the stretch with the sun overhead and not at either end of the day, and had not said why, and nobody had asked him to, because the reason was two miles of scrub and a buckle and did not need saying. They walked it quiet. The valley made its noise the whole length — insects, the dry thing in the thorn, the bird with two notes — and Cael counted the noise for once instead of the silence, and found no hole in it, and entered that as an observation and not as a finding. Something out here hunted Paths. Ruling: hold. He walked the hundred paces of bare gravel looking at the place where the scrub began again, and there was scrub in it.
