   207	*Forty-ninth day. Lowmarch. The lower inn, the table by the road door, a jug we can afford because of nine marks.*
   208	
   209	*Inventory, because I do one. Nine confirmed. One anomaly, session nine, still open, still real, patience. Deployment since the Line: none. Shadow tried again at dusk coming up from the landing, and I let it try, and it stopped. Anchor wants this doorway; it has wanted every doorway since the Ostrand road; I am letting it want. Eighteen. No injuries. Eighteen marks earned and about ten left after the jug.*
   210	
   211	*The board, as a system, because I would not trust it until I could see what it wanted. Hirers post — holds on good paper with a brand, brokers on thin paper with a clerk, carters in Pike's own hand because they can't write and he can. A crew marks the slip and the job is theirs, unless a hold has named a crew, and holds name the top of the wall. The crew goes and does the thing or doesn't. Whoever took delivery marks the slip and says what happened. No mark, no line. The slip comes back by any hand at all. Pike reads it aloud before it goes in, so the crew can say it's wrong, and reads it aloud after, so it's said — because half the meadow can't read and the book is his and the saying is theirs. Five columns. Then a wall, in his hand, ordered by him, on which a Bronze who lost a carter is under a Copper who brought one home.*
   212	
   213	*What it wants is for the people who hire to come back and hire again. That is the whole incentive. It runs everything — the price, the wall, the reading aloud. There is no other half. I looked for four days at Thornwater and I looked again today, and there is a man with one arm and a stone, and that is all of it.*
   214	
   215	*Where we are on it: last. Thirteen lines and ours is the thirteenth. Eleven between us and a crew called Teague's — four people, three years at the top, the ford and the autumn road both HOLD in his name. Pike says nobody knows us. He's right. Four lines, no losses, nobody hurt, every one of them a job the crews above us didn't want. I have been placed lower than that by people with less reason, and I have never once been placed by a man who told me the reason at the pen's pace while he wrote somebody else's line.*
   216	
   217	*No instrument out here reads me. No Arbiter station, no assessment panel, no registry seat with a seal on it. The board reads what we did. I've spent four years being the case the instruments couldn't parse, and it turns out the cure was a country with no instruments. Write it plainly, because it's true: I'm free. First time.*
