   228	
   229	The fourth came last and stopped in the door.
   230	
   231	He stopped the way a man stops when he has a thing he does every time and has never once skipped it. He did not look at the room. He did not look at the wall, or at Pike, or at the ledger under its stone. He looked at the tall woman, and then at the man with the crossbow, and then at the young one — each, in turn, for the length of a breath, the way Cael counted five at every halt — and only when he had them, all three, at the table with the jug, did he step in off the sill and let the door swing to behind him and begin, unhurried, to read the room.
   232	
   233	Force Path. Cael had it from the doorway, and checked it as the man came in: the weight down through the heels, the shoulders carried low and square, the hands loose — the same setting Pike sat in, except that this one had not stopped using it. Bronze, the book inside had said once, and would still say if anyone opened it, and nobody would. There was nothing on him a registry had given. No token, no cord, no colour. A face that had priced a great many things and kept none of the prices on it. He went to Pike first, and Pike said something short and put a slip in front of him — the ford's, Cael thought, by the size of the paper — and the man did not pick it up. He put two fingers on it, as Pike had put two fingers on the ledger, and left it where it was, and looked at his table, and only when the young one had food in front of him did he look back down at the slip and read it.
   234	
   235	Then he turned, with the slip still under his hand, and read the room properly, and the room included the five of them.
   236	
   237	Cael watched him do it. He knew the method because it was his own, and it was a strange thing to be on the far side of it. The eyes did not sweep; they stopped. They stopped on Lira, first, and went to her feet, and stayed there for the length of a breath, which was the correct place to look at Lira and the length of time it took to learn something. They went to Brom and did not linger, because Brom was a fact and facts did not take long. They went to Karis at the wall, with her notebook, copying, and something in the face moved by a degree — not a smile; the beginning of one, priced and put away. They came to the table, and to Cael, and passed him, and went looking for the fifth.
   238	
   239	He found Seln on the second pass. Most people did not find Seln on the third. Cael filed the speed and did not like it as much as he respected it.
   240	
   241	And then the eyes came back to him, and stayed, and Cael did the only honest thing available, which was the thing he was already doing: he read the man back.
   242	
   243	Item. He had counted his people before he had counted the job, and had done it at a door he had walked through a thousand times, with a slip on the table he had come in for. Item. He had gone to the ledger before he had gone to the jug. Item. He had found Seln. Item. He had priced Lira from her feet, which meant he had fought people who moved like Lira, or watched them, and knew where the information was. Item. He had looked at Cael last and longest, and Cael could not tell — this was the honest entry — whether it was because there was the most to read or the least.
   244	
   245	Neither of them spoke. There was nothing to say that the wall had not said, and both of them knew where the wall was.
   246	
   247	Teague looked a moment longer. Then he took his two fingers off the slip, and turned, and went back to his table, and sat down among his three with his back half to the room and his shoulder to the fire, and the tall woman said something to him, and he answered her, and the room's noise came back up to where it had been.
   248	
   249	Cael did not turn back to the jug yet.
   250	
   251	The landing door had not quite shut. The last of the day came through the gap in one flat bar and lay across the long wall, across the sheet pinned there at its four corners, and in that light he could read it from where he sat: the one word at the top, and their five names at the bottom, and between the two, in Pike's hand, eleven lines with the light on every one of them.

