     1	# Chapter 1 — Unmeasured
     2	
     3	*Second day past the Line.*
     4	
     5	*I'm dating this by the Line because there is nothing else out here to date it by. No bell, no term, no board with a week written on it. Karis says a calendar is an institution too, and she is right, so: two days.*
     6	
     7	*Walked eleven miles. The road is real. It has ruts, and the ruts are a cart's width. The carters call these the void roads. I have been reading that phrase off maps since I was fourteen — dashes, ending in nothing. It is a road. It has ruts.*
     8	
     9	*Inventory, because I do one at the start of anything. I am eighteen. Nine confirmed: Wind, Pressure, Iron, Compression, Ember, Storm, Anchor, Blade, and Shadow, which costs from waking whether I use it or not and drifted twice today on a road with nobody on it. Deployment since the Line: none. One anomaly. Session nine. Still open. Still real. Patience.*
    10	
    11	*No tier. No rank. No enrollment. And — I checked this morning from the top of the shelf, both directions, every morning for four years now — no file I can see.*
    12	
    13	*Fourth day. A ford.*
    14	
    15	*The last of the westbound couriers turned back here: a woman on a mule with a satchel of post, who took the two letters I have carried since the first camp and said this was as far as post went. Past the ford it is carters or nothing. She looked at the five of us and did not ask what we were. I waited for the question the whole time she was tightening her girth. It did not come. I am writing down that it did not come.*
    16	
    17	*Sixth day.*
    18	
    19	*Fell in behind three carts going east and watched them. The carters walk quiet. Not silent — quiet: no talk on the road, no singing, a word to the mule at need. Steel where it can be reached: a crossbow across the sacks, a bill-hook by the driver's knee. No fire until the road is behind them for the night, and then a small one. And when a boy on the second cart — sixteen, maybe — put his hand out toward a hillside as though he meant to try something, the driver knocked the hand down without looking round and said, "Not on the road."*
    20	
    21	*He did not say why. I asked at the fire. He said, "You're paper, aren't you," which is what they call anyone from inside the Line, and I said we were, and he said, "Then you know how to keep quiet. Keep it." I have had longer conversations with clerks and learned less.*
    22	
    23	*So, the etiquette, as observed and not as explained: walk quiet. Carry steel. Don't light anything you don't need. Don't declare. Nobody has given me the reason for any of it and I am not going to make one up. Claim: there is a reason. Evidence: three carters and a boy's hand knocked down. Ruling: hold.*
    24	
    25	*Ninth day. Lowmarch.*
    26	
    27	*A river, wide and brown, with a ford marked by poles and a rope ferry for the deep part. Two inns, priced for people with more coin than we have. A smith, working. No wall. A meadow above the ford full of carts and tents, which is where anyone who is not a carter sleeps, and where we are. The ferryman told Brom that half the faces in the meadow will be gone by the month's end and the other half will be new. Nobody here has been here long enough to be anybody.*
    28	
    29	*The board is under a roof on four posts by the ferry landing: planks nailed to the posts at head height, slips of paper pinned to the planks. I read it like the Cinder House board at fourteen: all of it, twice, and then sorted.*
    30	
    31	*Fourteen postings. Six escorts. Three clearances — two fence-lines, one ford. Two recoveries — a cart in a wash, a load off a bank. Three hold contracts, priced high, with a word on each in the board-keeper's hand: HOLD, meaning a crew is on it. Every slip prices the job. Not one slip prices the crew. There is no line on any of them for what a crew has to be — no "Iron or above," no "certified," nothing — and I looked for it the way you look for a stair in the dark, with a foot, because every board I have ever read had it.*
    32	
    33	*Sorted by what each would cost the five of us: the escorts cost time. The fence-lines cost Brom's back. The wash costs rope and Lira. The ford clearance I can't price, because I don't know what is in the ford.*
    34	
    35	*Pike keeps the board. Sixties. One arm — the left sleeve pinned flat by someone who has done it every morning for years and stopped thinking about it. He sits like a Force practitioner who has stopped using it, weight all the way down. He said "Copper, Force, thirty years on the roads" in the same breath as his name, as a man says his trade, and did not ask ours. He asked what we could do. Lira said "Walk, carry, climb, fight," and he said "In that order?" and she said "Depends on the day," and he wrote something down.*
    36	
    37	*He opened a heading for us. Five names down the margin of a fresh page, in his hand, spelled as we say them. Five columns ruled beside the names: Job. Hirer. Witness. Outcome. Price. I read it upside down, because I read everything upside down, and then read it again to be sure I had not missed the column every ledger I have ever been entered in has had, the one that says what you are. I had not missed it. The page does not have it.*
    38	
    39	*Fourteenth day.*
    40	
    41	*First contract. Ferry escort: a day on the ferry, six crossings, with a broker's bales and a broker who wanted five people visible on his goods. Nothing happened. Two marks for standing where we could be seen, which is a job I have done before, in a gallery, at a higher price and for worse people. Pike's line, read upside down: escort, ferry, six crossings, no loss, two marks. The broker made his mark under "witness." The line does not say who we are. It says what we did.*
    42	
    43	*Twenty-second day.*
    44	
    45	*Second contract. Fence-line clearance at a steading a half-day north — a mile of posts choked in thorn, too few people to cut it before the stock came down. Three days, bill-hooks and gloves. Brom cut for six hours without stopping. Karis lit nothing; she was asked not to, and did not ask why, and wrote the not-asking in her notebook, underlined. Four marks and a sack of flour, and the steading's woman said "Cheap," to nobody, in the tone of a woman who intends to hire us again.*
    46	
    47	*Thirty-third day.*
    48	
    49	*Third contract. A carter's cart gone into a wash on the north road, nose down, one wheel in the water. Rope. Lira on the far bank because she is the one who can get to a far bank; Brom on the near one because he is the one who can hold it. Two hours, and the cart came out with three wheels. Three marks. The carter looked at Brom for a while afterward and said "You're not paper," and Brom said "I am," and the carter shrugged and paid.*
    50	
    51	*Three contracts. Nine marks and some flour. Nobody has been hurt and nobody has been asked anything. The unit is finding its feet in a country that has never seen an academy, and the feet are slow, and the feet are ours.*
    52	
    53	*Note, because a thing I keep noticing goes in the log: nobody is watching. I have counted every morning — the far bank, the meadow's edge, the road both ways — and the count is zero, and I keep counting, because the counting is the last thing the watchers left me and I have not decided yet whether to put it down.*
    54	
    55	---
    56	
    57	The fourth contract came to their fire instead of the other way round, on the forty-first evening, in the shape of a man who had asked Pike where the cheap crew slept.
    58	
    59	Cael had him catalogued before he sat. Fifty, or a road's version of fifty. A face weather had done most of the work on. Hands that had learned exactly two things, a rein and a wheel, and had stopped taking on new ones. A way of standing that kept his mule between himself and anything he had not priced yet, which at present was the five of them. The mule looked at Brom, because animals looked at Brom.
    60	
    61	"Pike says you're five," the carter said, "and cheap."
    62	
    63	"We're five," said Lira.
    64	
    65	"Thornwater. Grain, one cart, two days. Nine marks and the hold feeds you." He looked at the fire rather than at any of them. "There's a stretch on the second day. Road drops into the scrub for two miles before the fence. Two carts went quiet on it last month. One we found. Cart, mule, no driver. The other we didn't."
    66	
    67	"Quiet," said Karis.
    68	
    69	"Quiet."
    70	
    71	She waited for the rest. There was no rest. Cael watched her decide not to ask a second time, which for Karis was a physical act.
    72	
    73	"You walk ahead of me," the carter said. "Not far. Quiet — no talk on the stretch. Nothing lit. Steel where you can reach it. And you don't declare. Not on that stretch. Not for anything."
    74	
    75	"What if something—" Lira began.
    76	
    77	"You don't declare."
    78	
    79	From the far side of the fire, where he had been sitting long enough that the carter had not counted him, Seln said, "Understood."
    80	
    81	The carter looked at Seln for the first time, and nodded once. "Sunrise," he said. "The meadow gate. Cart's got room on top of the sacks if you've a load."
    82	
    83	They had a load. Karis's three boxes went up onto the grain at first light, roped by Brom and checked by Karis at every halt for two days, and Seln's locked case rode where it always rode, on Seln.
    84	
    85	---
    86	
    87	The stretch was the last two miles of the road, and the carter had timed the day so they would reach it with light to spare, and the day had not cooperated. A wheel had found a stone on the morning's bench and taken an hour and a wedge to answer for it. So they came down off the shelf into the valley with the sun already behind the hills at their backs and the light going the grey that light went when it had stopped being anyone's.
    88	
    89	Cael read the ground as he would have read a floor.
    90	
    91	The road ran east, straight, down a long shallow valley whose floor was scrub — grey-green, thorned, knee-high near the road and chest-high thirty paces off it, dense on the right-hand side and thinning to the left, where the ground fell away in gravel to a dry wash. The wash ran beside the road the whole length of the stretch, ten or twelve paces off. On the right there was nothing but scrub to the valley wall, a quarter mile of it, dark already. Two miles ahead, where the valley closed, there was a line that was not scrub, and one light on it.
    92	
    93	The order had settled itself on the first day without a word from anyone, because two years of walking into rooms together had made it a habit. Lira went first, twenty paces ahead, on the balls of her feet. Cael and Karis came next, five paces ahead of the mule, on the crown of the road. Brom walked at the mule's near shoulder with one hand on the cheek-strap, because the carter had asked for a big man at the mule's head and had not said why, and because the mule had decided about Brom on the first morning. The carter walked at the off side with the rein. Seln was on the left, off the road, along the wash's lip, in the place two years had taught Cael to look for him last.
    94	
    95	The valley was making noise. He noticed that only afterward, when it stopped — the small continuous racket of a scrubland at dusk, insects, something dry moving in the thorn, a bird somewhere on the right saying the same two notes at intervals. The mule's harness had its own noise, a leather creak and a buckle that had not been set, which Cael had stopped trying to stop hearing.
    96	
    97	They had gone perhaps half a mile into the stretch when Lira stopped and put her hand up.
    98	
    99	She did not turn. Her hand went up open, palm back, the way it went up on a floor when she wanted a drill halted and did not want to spend breath on it, and she stood where she had stopped with her weight forward and did not move anything else.
   100	
   101	Brom stopped the mule. The carter said nothing. Karis, beside Cael, went still in the way that meant something had happened and she did not yet know what.
   102	
   103	Cael listened for it, and did not hear it, and then understood that this was the point.
   104	
   105	The right-hand side of the valley had gone silent. Not the whole valley — the wash side still ticked and whirred, the bird was still there somewhere behind them — but from the road to the valley wall on the right, for as far ahead as he could judge, the scrub had stopped making any sound at all. It was the shape of the silence Lira had heard. A hole in the noise, and the hole was keeping pace with them.
   106	
   107	From the lip of the wash, without turning his head toward the scrub, without raising his voice above the pitch of the road, Seln said: "Don't declare anything."
   108	
   109	Nobody answered him. Nobody needed to.
   110	
   111	"Walk," Seln said. "Same pace."
   112	
   113	Lira's hand came down. She walked. The mule walked, because Brom did. Cael walked, and did the thing he had trained himself to do on floors where the other man had already moved and he had not yet seen how, which was to catalogue instead of react.
   114	
   115	He found it on the second look. Forty meters off the road on the right, in the chest-high scrub, a place where the grey was a different grey — lower than the thorn, longer than a dog, so exactly the valley's colour that he lost it the moment he looked straight at it and found it again only at the edge of his eye. It was moving. It was moving when they moved, at their pace, and when he watched it for fifty paces it neither gained a stride nor lost one.
   116	
   117	Forty meters. He knew forty meters as well as he knew his own stride; it was the long side of the Ironyard floor, and he had chalked it himself once, at fifteen.
   118	
   119	Claim: it is alone. Evidence: one shape; one hole in the noise, no wider than one animal makes. Ruling: unproven. He looked left, at the wash side, for a second hole, and did not find one, and did not let that become a finding.
   120	
   121	He walked.
   122	
   123	Lira, twenty paces ahead, was walking the way she walked on a floor in the last minute before a bout: everything in her wanting to go, nothing in her going. He knew what she could do to it. One burst — Wind, her own, Iron Rank One by an instrument's account — and the forty meters of scrub between her and it would lie flat, and the thing would either run or be found. She had said as much to nobody in the set of her shoulders. Then she said it in words, low, not turning.
   124	
   125	"I could clear it."
   126	
   127	"No," said Seln.
   128	
   129	"I know. I'm saying it so I've said it."
   130	
   131	Nobody said anything after that for two hundred paces.
   132	
   133	Brom's hand had not moved on the mule's cheek-strap. Cael had watched Brom answer anything with teeth for three years, and the answer had always been the same: to become the thing teeth broke on. He was not becoming it. He was walking at a mule's shoulder in the near dark with his shoulders down and his breathing slow, exactly as large as his body made him and not one degree larger, and once, when the buckle jingled, he said "Easy" to the mule in a voice that carried nowhere at all.
   134	
   135	"Mule's fine," he said, a little later, to the carter, at the same pitch.
   136	
   137	The carter had a crossbow across the sacks and had not touched it.
   138	
   139	Karis was the one Cael watched. She had never seen a large animal she did not want to document. She walked with her right hand at her coat, where the notebook lived, and Cael saw her decide not to take it out four separate times in a hundred paces. She could have lit the scrub. One point of ignition at contact, one channel, and the whole right side of the valley would have been a wall the thing had to go round, and she would have had a sample by its light. Her hand went to the coat a fifth time and stopped there.
   140	
   141	"No light," Seln said, from the wash, before she had asked.
   142	
   143	"I want to see it."
   144	
   145	"Then see it."
   146	
   147	She looked. He watched her look, and count under her breath, and file.
   148	
   149	And Cael held the nine.
   150	
   151	Holding was never one act; it was a sequence of small refusals arriving faster than a person could name them. The Iron-adjacent read was the cheapest thing he owned and it wanted the ground under the thing's feet, wanted its weight and its gait through the surface of the valley, and he did not give it the ground. Anchor, which had been wanting fixed points in ordinary doorways since a road outside Ostrand, found the wash's near lip beside him and wanted a line laid along it, and he let it want. Shadow, which had been trying to switch itself on at dusk every evening since the Line, tried, and he held it off as he had held it off for three hours once in a room off a records hall, under the heaviest observation of his life. Wind sat where Wind sat. Storm sat where Storm sat, heavy. None of them moved.
   152	
   153	What he did instead was the oldest thing he had. He looked.
   154	
   155	The observation, at full depth, on a floor, was a way of taking a fighter apart into what the fighter did and did not do and finding the seam between the two. He had never once run it on an animal. He ran it now, at a walking pace, in failing light, on a shape he could not look at directly, and found that the discipline did not care what it was pointed at.
   156	
   157	Item. It kept its distance. Forty meters at the half-mile, forty at the six hundredth pace, forty when Lira slowed for a rut and forty when she found her pace again. It did not close. It did not fall back. It was not stalking; stalking closed. It was keeping station.
   158	
   159	Item. It made no sound. Not the scrub-sound an animal that size should have made moving through thorn — he listened for it specifically, the rasp and give of branches, and there was none. It moved through the thorn as though the thorn had agreed.
   160	
   161	Item. It tracked the mule. By the thousandth pace he was sure of it: when the buckle jingled, the shape's front — its head, if that was a head — turned toward the road; when Brom spoke to the mule, it did not. When Lira's boot found a stone and sent it into the wash with a clatter, it did not turn to Lira. It turned to the cart. It was reading the loudest thing on the road, and the loudest thing on the road was leather and a buckle.
   162	
   163	Item. It did not cross open ground. He learned this at the place, three-quarters of a mile in, where the scrub on the right gave out for a hundred paces — a gravel fan off the valley wall, bare, pale, nothing on it a dog could stand behind. The shape came to the edge of the scrub and stopped. They walked the hundred paces with the right-hand side empty and the noise of the valley still switched off, and at the far edge, where the thorn began again, it was there. Forty meters off. Keeping pace. He had been looking the whole hundred paces and had not seen it cross, and there was nothing on that fan a cat could have hidden behind, and so: claim, it went round, behind the scrub-line, out of his sight. Evidence: none. Ruling: it went round, which meant it had a way round, which meant it knew this ground as the carter knew the road.
   164	
   165	Item — and this was the one he wrote down first that night, because it was the only one that was about them and not about it. It reacted to nothing they did. It had stopped when Lira stopped and walked when she walked and turned its head to a buckle, and in a mile of road it had done nothing in answer to five people, because in a mile of road five people had done nothing to answer.
   166	
   167	That was the whole tactic. He saw it whole at about the thousandth pace, walking, with his hands empty and his eyes on a grey that would not be looked at: the thing was waiting for something, and the something was the one thing the carters did not do, and the reason the carter had hired five strangers who were paper was not their steel and not their backs. It was that paper knew how to be quiet. He had been quiet in front of a hearing. He had been quiet in front of a delegation. He had held nine things to nothing in front of every instrument the continent owned, and the instruments had marked him down for it, and here was a valley where it was the only competence that priced.
   168	
   169	Lira stopped once more, a few hundred paces on, for a breath, to see if it would; and it did.
   170	
   171	"Don't test it," said Seln.
   172	
   173	"I wasn't."
   174	
   175	"Then don't stop."
   176	
   177	She went on. The light was nearly gone now, the scrub on the right one grey and the sky over it another, and the line at the valley's end had become a fence with two lamps on it and a gate between them. He could hear the fence before he could see its posts: a dog, the ordinary kind, barking behind it, at intervals, at nothing.
   178	
   179	A hundred meters short of the fence — he paced it the next morning, because he was who he was — the shape stopped. They walked on and it did not. He looked back from the last low rise before the gate and there was nothing at the edge of the scrub but scrub, and the right-hand side of the valley, behind them, had begun to make its small noises again.
   180	
   181	It had gone the way it came. He had not seen it go either.
   182	
   183	---
   184	
   185	Thornwater at the gate, catalogued while a woman with a lantern looked them over: a fence of split timber, close-set, twice a man's height, running left and right into the dark farther than the lamps reached; the gate two leaves of the same timber, barred, with a walk above it where a man stood with a bow and did not point it; behind the fence, roofs, four or five, low and thatched, and the smell of animals and a bake-oven and wet earth. A steading. The dog he had heard was on a chain inside and had stopped barking the moment the gate-woman spoke to it, which told him more about the place than the fence did.
   186	
   187	"Trouble?" the woman said. She said it to the carter. She had looked at the five of them for exactly as long as it took to count them.
   188	
   189	The carter had brought the mule up to the gate and stopped it, and he answered in the register of a man reporting a road he had walked for twenty years, which was to say without any weight at all.
   190	
   191	"No," he said. "First crew I've hired that knew to shut up."
   192	
   193	Cael took it and put it where he kept such things. Rooke's three words. Vell's first line in a bound book. A woman's single nod at a standings board. There were not many. This one went in beside them without having to be argued in, and he noticed himself not arguing, and let it stand.
   194	
   195	The gate opened. The mule went through it as a mule goes through a gate it knows.
   196	
   197	Karis stopped the carter with a word before he could follow.
   198	
   199	"What is it," Karis said. "On the stretch."
   200	
   201	The carter looked at her. He was not unkind about it. He was a man being asked, at the end of a road, to describe the road.
   202	
   203	"Stillhound," he said.
   204	
   205	"And it—"
   206	
   207	"It's a stillhound. You shut up. It went home." He took up the slack in the rein. "Twenty years. That's all I've got, and I've had it a while."
   208	
   209	He went in. Karis stood at the gate for a moment with the word, holding it as she held a line copied out of a charter she could not yet read, and then she followed the cart, and the gate-woman looked at Cael, last through, with the lantern up.
   210	
   211	"You're paper," she said.
   212	
   213	"Yes."
   214	
   215	"Hm." It was neither a question nor a verdict. She barred the gate behind him. On the walk above, the man with the bow had already turned back to the road, to the dark where the scrub was, and was looking at it as the carter had looked at the fire: as a fact about the country that did not need his opinion.
   216	
   217	The carter had a slip. He took it from inside his coat, folded to the size of a palm, and gave it to the gate-woman, and she unfolded it against the gatepost and read it by her lantern — Pike's hand, the posting copied out, five ruled lines beneath it — and made her mark under the fourth, and said "Delivered. No losses," aloud, because that was plainly the procedure, and gave it back. It would go back to Lowmarch in his coat, and Pike would write what it said.
   218	
   219	Cael, reading upside down from four feet away, had seen the fourth line's heading before the lantern moved. Outcome. Not a word beside it about who had walked.
