     1	# Chapter 1 — The Valley With No Name
     2	
     3	*Second day past the Line. One fire. Deployment since the Line: none.*
     4	
     5	He had written that under the inventory an hour ago, and since then he had done nothing at all but keep it true.
     6	
     7	The fire had burned down to a red eye, and Cael let it go, for Brom had said at the start of the watch that a man who feeds a fire all night ends by looking at the fire, and Brom was generally right about anything that had weight. So he sat with his back to the embers and his face to the dark, on a shelf of turf that nobody had ever troubled to name, and he counted.
     8	
     9	It was an old habit, and he had it from a hill. For a year there had been two men at the foot of a road with a tin cup between them, and a third on a tier with a pencil, and a mild clerk at a copying table who had turned out to be the best of the four; and he had learned to find each of them in the first minute of any morning, as a man feels for his purse. He ran the count now over a country he could not see. He took the ridge behind them first, and the scree, and the black shoulder to the north where a night bird kept its two notes, and then the gulf in front, in sections, from the near ground outward.
