     5	It was not unfriendly, for Cael had been ignored by experts, in a house on a bluff, and he knew the feel of it; this was something else, and he came to think of it as being let alone. On the eighth evening the woman with the hides walked back along the road at dusk and laid a flat cake on the shaft of their cart, still warm from being carried inside her coat, and walked away again before anybody could thank her. Lira broke it in five, and Brom said it was barley, and nobody found anything to add.
     6	
     7	The country came down as they went. The red trees thinned and gave over to a broad pale tree with leaves that showed silver when the wind turned them, which Karis also declined to name, and the river they were following grew slow and wide and took in others. It was warm in the bottom. The dust stood behind the carts until long after they had passed, and there were flies about the gelding's eyes all day.
