   295	It was a dry stream cut deep through red earth, twelve feet down and thirty across at the bottom, with banks too steep to stand on. The road went into it by a ramp dug out of the eastern side and climbed out by another on the west. A trickle ran in the bed, no wider than a hand. Karis looked at that trickle before she looked at anything else, and went down and drove a peeled stick into the gravel at its edge, and cut a notch in the stick at the waterline with her knife.
   296	
   297	The wagon lay at the foot of the far ramp. It was a long four-wheeled thing, and it had come down from the east loaded with baled hides. Its near front wheel had found a boulder. The wheel was a hub and a handful of splinters, and the wagon had slewed off the ramp's foot and settled on that corner, canted over, with its tail up the slope behind it.
   298	
   299	The woman with the hides was on the western lip with her two mules. She had got a third of her load up the ramp by herself, a bale at a time, and she was grey to the hair with dust. Cael knew her, for she had once laid a barley cake on the shaft of their cart, and she had been standing ten feet away when an old man asked him what he was. She pointed at the bed of the wash with her whole hand, and that was her greeting.
