   302	
   303	They made a chain of it and had the bales out in half an hour, and while the last of them went up, Brom walked round the wagon as he walked round any load. When he came back he had the whole of it, and he gave it to them with his hands as much as his voice.
   304	
   305	A pole went under the broken corner, lashed fore and aft, so that the wagon would ride on three wheels and a skid. The long rope went from the tongue up the western ramp to the mules, and to Brom. That was the haul. But the bed fell away downstream, and a thing dragged across a slope will always swing its tail to the low side and go over; so a second line must run from the tail-ring back to the eastern bank, and somebody must stand there and pay it out, a foot at a time, and never let it go slack.
   306	
