   223	That was the method. He had used it on fighters for two years, from the east tier of a yard, and he had never once tried it on an animal; but he could find no reason why it should not hold. One wrote down what could be counted. One gave a name to nothing else.
   224	
   225	So, first: it kept its station. He checked that three times. The ruts bent a little to the right, toward the swell, and the grey shape gave ground, drifting up the slope until there were forty-five yards between them again. The ruts bent left, and it came down. Whatever it wanted, it wanted it from exactly that far away, like a watcher who has been told his distance.
   226	
   227	Second: it made no sound. He had known that from the grass. Now he saw the other half of it, which was that the frogs went dumb a little way in front of it and took up again a little way behind, so that it walked the whole time inside the hole that Lira had heard. He did not know how frogs had learned to do that. He thought it would take a long time.
   228	
   229	The third thing he nearly missed. Its head was not turned toward the people. All the way up the valley it had been turned a little behind him, toward the cart, and when he looked for the reason he heard it: the buckle. It was the one thing in the whole party that kept time. He lifted his left hand and closed the fist, twice, where Brom would see.
   230	
   231	There was a pause of four strides. Then the clicking stopped, for a large hand had been reached over the tailboard and shut on a strap.
   232	
   233	The grey head swung. It went slowly to one side and then to the other, low, casting, as a dog casts that has lost a line. Brom let go. The buckle clicked, and the head came round and settled on it again.
   234	
   235	*It hears. As a dog does.* That was worth having, though not for itself. It could hear a buckle at forty-five yards, and so it had certainly heard a wired wheel, and ten feet walking, and a carter breathing through his teeth. It had heard all of them for half a mile, and it had done nothing. Whatever it was waiting for was not a sound that they were making.
   236	
   237	The fourth thing was the gravel.
   238	
   239	A gully came down out of the right-hand hill, and at its foot the floods of other years had spread a fan of pale stones clean across the valley, a hundred feet wide, with a skin of water shining on it. The ruts ran straight over. The mule's feet rattled in the wet, and the cart came after with a noise like a sack of nails. Out on the right the grey shape came to the edge of the fan and stopped as if it had met a wall.
   240	
   241	It would not put a foot on it. It turned, and for the first time it ran, a long flat lope up the slope and round the head of the gully, where the stones began; he lost it against the hill, and found it, sixty yards off, and seventy. Then it came down the far side at the same lope, and dropped to a walk, and it was forty-five yards from the road again before the tailboard was clear of the wet.
   242	
   243	He did not know why. He set that down, in his head, with a ruled line under it, for Karis.
   244	
   245	The fifth thing was not a thing it did, and it took him the longest to see.
   246	
   247	It had done nothing. It had answered nothing. For a mile it had gone beside six people and a mule like a man kept waiting outside a door. Such a man has been told that a word will be said, and that he is to do nothing until he hears it. They had not said the word. He did not know what the word was. But he had put down nine hands at every stride for a mile, and Lira had held her feet, and Karis her pencil, and Brom had been only a large man with a hedging tool. Not one Path had been declared on that road, and not one fragment had answered; and so the thing had been given nothing, and had reacted to nothing.
   248	
   249	There was a light in front of them.
   250	
   251	The fence stood up out of the grass all at once, as the carter had said it would: split posts, as high as a man's head, from one shoulder of the valley to the other, with a gate in the middle and a lantern coming toward the gate from inside. He heard a bar drawn.
   252	
   253	He looked to the right. The grey shape had stopped.
   254	
   255	It had not sat, or turned. It stood in the thin grass with its head level, beside a white stone, and it came no farther. Cael fixed the stone. Then he counted his own strides from abreast of it to the gate, as Karis would have counted, with his lips shut.
   256	
   257	A hundred and ten.
   258	
   259	Nobody had struck a blow, or so much as drawn a knife. Behind him he heard Brom let out a breath that he seemed to have been keeping since the mouth of the valley. The gate swung inward on a leather hinge, and the lantern came up, and behind it he saw a woman's hand.

