   215	Cael wrote in the loft door that evening, with his legs hanging over the yard and the last of the sun on the page.
   216	
   217	He had not written much at Thornwater. The first night he had not trusted his hand, and since then he had drawn water, and carried what Brom could not, and helped Karis to pace out a number along a fence. He was eighteen, and for three days he had been a hired man on a grain hold, and it had suited him better than he would have guessed. But there was a scrap of bark in the pocket of Hesk's book now, where two letters had lain, and it seemed to him that the book ought to know.
   218	
   219	*Forty-sixth day past the Line. Thornwater: a grain hold at the head of a valley, two days east of the board. Fourth line, in Pike's hand, copied and brought up by the carter. Nine marks, in his box. Earned, eighteen. In hand tonight, a few coppers and a map.*
